# A144 + A214 — the snapshot carries attachment metadata, not attachment bytes (2026-09-15)

# The same four tests, run against the code before the change and after it. The "before" numbers come
# from a `git worktree` of the parent commit (27e8f9e) with only the test file added, so the comparison
# is the same measurement on the same machine rather than arithmetic from the finding.

## BEFORE — `imageBase64: document.imageData?.base64EncodedString()` in `snapshot()`
$ swift test --filter AttachmentPayloadTests          # in the worktree at 27e8f9e
  ✔ Test "The bytes the model is sent are still the engine's own copy" passed
  ✘ Test "The snapshot carries an attachment's metadata and not its bytes"
      Expectation failed: data.count < image.count
      the snapshot is 100133 bytes for a 65536-byte image, so it is carrying it
  ✘ Test "One maximum-size attachment leaves the snapshot inside the transport's cap"
      Expectation failed: data.count < 1_000_000
      the snapshot is 89489877 bytes for a 67108864-byte attachment
  ✘ Test "Two maximum-size attachments still leave it inside the cap"
      Expectation failed: data.count < ProtocolLimits.maximumMessageBytes
      the snapshot is 178968510 bytes against a 89544022-byte cap, so the engine cannot send it at all
  ✘ Test run with 4 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.802 seconds with 3 issues.

# The two-attachment line is A214 exactly: 178 968 510 bytes against the 89 544 022-byte cap, which is
# `(64 MiB * 4 + 2) / 3 + 64 KiB`. An over-cap message is not a slow push — the transmitter refuses to
# frame it, so the state update is dropped and the client keeps the previous state.

## AFTER — the snapshot carries the id, name, kind, summary, tokens and truncation flag
$ swift test --filter AttachmentPayloadTests
MEASURE small-image snapshot:     11366 bytes
MEASURE one-max-attachment:       11372 bytes
MEASURE two-max-attachment:       11500 bytes, cap: 89544022
  ✔ Test run with 4 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.033 seconds.

# 178 968 510 -> 11 500 bytes for the case that was refused, and the snapshot's size no longer depends
# on what is attached: 64 KiB and 2 x 64 MiB land 134 bytes apart. Four tests, three of which fail
# against the old shape, and the fourth — the engine still holding the bytes for the model request —
# passes on both sides, which is what says the change removed a copy rather than the capability.

# Two notes for whoever reads this next. The first version of the "not its bytes" test searched the
# encoded snapshot for the image's base64 text and **passed against the old code**: Foundation's JSON
# encoder writes `/` as `\/`, so a needle containing a slash is not found in the haystack it is in. The
# assertion is a size comparison now, which cannot be fooled that way. And one maximum-size image did
# fit the cap before the change — the cap *is* one attachment plus its envelope — so that test asserts
# what actually changed: the snapshot no longer scales with the attachments.

